okay today I'm going to talk to you  about enzyme graphs these are just  simply graphs associated with enzyme  activity as a very quick recap enzymes  of biological catalysts that's something  which speeds up a rate of reaction  without being used of itself the act on  substrate molecules the substrate  molecule fits into the active site of an  enzyme in a complementary way and gets  broken down to form products so what  we're going to look at is how three  different things affect enzyme activity  and we're going to see the graph  associated with each one so the first  one we're going to look at is  temperature temperature so with each of  the graphs up the y-axis we have rate of  reaction all this means is how fast the  enzyme reaction is happening so on  the temperature graph the shape of the  graph looks very much like this it goes  up and then it comes back down again and  it gets to zero here now along the  x-axis we have temperature because at  this side of the x-axis we have a very  low temperature and quite a low rate of  reaction as we increase the temperature  we have an increase in rate of reaction  but as we keep on increasing the  temperature the rate of reaction Falls  until it's zero so why does this happen  so initially there is an increase of  rate of reaction just like in any  chemical reaction because as we heat up  these particles to substrate in the  enzyme we give them more kinetic energy  so there's going to be more collisions  between our substrate and our active  site this keeps on going until we reach  something which is called the optimum  temperature this particular temperature  optimum temperature and this particular  temperature the enzymes are working most  efficiently now in the human body  is usually around 37 degrees Celsius  however if we keep on increasing the  temperature the rate of reaction starts  to drop now the reason why this is is  due to the active site that intense heat  breaks down the active site which means  the substrate can no longer fit in  because it can't fit in anymore no more  substrate can be broken down and  therefore we will have a slower rate of  reaction this process is called  denaturing so the hotter it gets the  more and more enzymes are denatured  therefore the slower the rate of  reaction the next thing we're going to  look at is the effect of pH on rate of  reaction just like before up the y-axis  where you're going to show rate of  reaction but this time across the x-axis  we are going to show we're going to show  the pH remember pH is just the measure  of how acid or how alkaline something is  on our pH scale remember a pH of one  would be very acidic a pH of seven is  neutral and a pH of fourteen would be  very alkaline so pH has a very similar  shape of graph to temperature so we can  see that each enzyme will have an  optimum pH for example this one here  could have an optimum pH of around seven  now the reason why the the rate of  reaction is very slow either side is  again the how acid or alkaline it is can  affect the bonds in the active site of  the enzyme and therefore how well the  substrate can be broken down however  different enzymes have  for an optimum ph 'as the an enzyme in  your saliva in your mouth for example  might have the best have an optimum pH  of seven whereas you could have an  enzyme in your stomach that could have  an optimum pH of around one or one or  two so it's a very similar idea to the  temperature graphs for pH but only  remember that different enzymes work  best at different PHS you can even have  one working best at an alkali pH so the  last one we're going to look at is we  are going to look at the effect of  concentration of substrate so remember  concentration basically means how many  of those particles we have within a  particular volume now this one has an  interesting shape it starts to increase  like this but then it reaches a maximum  and levels out like this so why does  this happen let me just draw a little  diagram to show how this works so here  are three enzymes and I'm going to draw  a substrate molecule okay so when we  have a low substrate concentration let's  say if this is shown here because I've  just got one um but there's not going to  be many chance of collisions remember  these are going to be moving around and  the chances of a collision are  relatively low because we only have one  substrate particle molecule here however  if we were to increase the concentration  of substrate molecules as I can show  right here our chances of a collision  between our enzymes active site and our  substrate increases therefore we've got  we've got an increased chance of the  substrate being broken down therefore we  have a faster  rate of reaction so why is it that it  levels out after a while now the reason  for that is this let's say we keep on  increasing the substrate concentration  and keep on going  after a while we can see didn't do that  very well  after oil we can see that all of these  active sites are occupied at any one  time so every single one of these active  sites is brick busy breaking down a  substrate molecule this means that it  doesn't matter how many more substrate  molecules we were introduced it's  working at its maximum rate so all of  the active sites are full so that is how  these three different things affect  enzyme activity the final one remember  was constant substrate concentration  thank you
 

